1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,297
Here's a question that sounds easy.

2
00:00:02,617 --> 00:00:05,262
How many different games
of tic-tac-toe are there?

3
00:00:05,467 --> 00:00:11,393
By a game, we mean the whole
list of moves, in order.

4
00:00:11,713 --> 00:00:15,678
So if two games end with the
same board, but the moves happened

5
00:00:15,678 --> 00:00:19,202
in a different order, they
count as two different games.

6
00:00:19,522 --> 00:00:23,090
Flipped or turned versions
count separately too.

7
00:00:24,133 --> 00:00:25,908
Take a guess. A hundred?

8
00:00:26,228 --> 00:00:30,244
A million? Pause the video
and write your guess down.

9
00:00:38,467 --> 00:00:41,259
The answer is 255,168.

10
00:00:41,579 --> 00:00:46,269
In this video we'll find out where
that number comes from: first by careful

11
00:00:46,269 --> 00:00:51,147
counting, and then with a short computer
program that plays every single game.

12
00:00:52,132 --> 00:00:54,079
Let's start with a simpler question.

13
00:00:54,399 --> 00:00:57,343
Suppose the players
ignore three in a row, and

14
00:00:57,343 --> 00:01:00,415
just keep going until all
nine squares are full.

15
00:01:00,735 --> 00:01:03,486
In how many different orders
can the board fill up?

16
00:01:03,732 --> 00:01:06,288
X goes first and has 9
squares to choose from.

17
00:01:06,608 --> 00:01:09,928
For each of those, O has 8 squares left.

18
00:01:10,248 --> 00:01:14,187
That's 9 groups of 8, so the
choices multiply: 9 times

19
00:01:14,187 --> 00:01:17,908
8 is 72 different ways to
make the first two moves.

20
00:01:18,132 --> 00:01:23,052
Keep going: 9 times 8 times 7,
and so on, all the way down to 1.

21
00:01:23,372 --> 00:01:26,464
Mathematicians have a
short name for this: nine

22
00:01:26,464 --> 00:01:29,820
factorial, written as a 9
with an exclamation mark.

23
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:32,837
It equals 362,880.

24
00:01:33,065 --> 00:01:34,514
But wait.

25
00:01:34,834 --> 00:01:37,835
That's more than 255,168.

26
00:01:38,155 --> 00:01:39,814
Our count is too high.

27
00:01:40,134 --> 00:01:43,064
Where are all those
extra games coming from?

28
00:01:44,661 --> 00:01:45,802
Here's the catch.

29
00:01:46,122 --> 00:01:50,598
Real tic-tac-toe stops as soon
as someone gets three in a row.

30
00:01:50,918 --> 00:01:56,492
In this game, X wins on the fifth move,
so the last four squares are never played.

31
00:01:56,728 --> 00:02:00,489
But our 362,880 counted those missing moves

32
00:02:00,489 --> 00:02:04,949
anyway, as if the players
kept going after the win.

33
00:02:05,269 --> 00:02:11,392
The 4 empty squares can be filled in
4 times 3 times 2 times 1, so 24 ways.

34
00:02:11,712 --> 00:02:14,387
So this one game got counted 24 times!

35
00:02:14,707 --> 00:02:18,121
Let's call those made-up
endings ghost games.

36
00:02:18,441 --> 00:02:21,791
We want to count each real game only once.

37
00:02:21,994 --> 00:02:24,128
So when can a game end?

38
00:02:24,448 --> 00:02:25,980
Not before move 5.

39
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:29,480
X plays moves 1, 3 and 5, so the fifth move

40
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:32,882
is the first time anyone
can have three marks.

41
00:02:33,128 --> 00:02:37,726
Pause and ponder: how many games
end with X winning on move 5?

42
00:02:38,046 --> 00:02:40,364
Try to count them before I show you.

43
00:03:00,594 --> 00:03:02,910
Here's one way, in three steps.

44
00:03:03,230 --> 00:03:06,044
First, pick the line X wins with.

45
00:03:06,364 --> 00:03:10,333
There are 8: 3 rows, 3
columns and 2 diagonals.

46
00:03:10,652 --> 00:03:15,039
Second, X fills that line
in some order: 3 times

47
00:03:15,039 --> 00:03:19,151
2 times 1, which is 3
factorial, so 6 orders.

48
00:03:19,471 --> 00:03:23,567
Third, O's first mark can go
on any of the other 6 squares,

49
00:03:23,567 --> 00:03:27,941
and O's second mark on any of
the 5 left: 6 times 5 is 30 ways.

50
00:03:28,261 --> 00:03:33,278
Multiply: 8 times 6
times 30 is 1,440 games.

51
00:03:35,125 --> 00:03:37,557
What about games that O wins on move 6?

52
00:03:37,877 --> 00:03:39,770
O needs three in a row.

53
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:44,441
But careful: if X's three marks
also make a line, X already

54
00:03:44,441 --> 00:03:48,717
won on move 5, and the game
stopped before O could finish.

55
00:03:48,925 --> 00:03:53,406
Use the same three steps: 8
lines for O, 6 orders for O's

56
00:03:53,406 --> 00:03:58,044
marks, and 6 times 5 times 4
ways to place X's three marks.

57
00:03:58,364 --> 00:04:02,559
That's 5,760 ways for O to finish a line.

58
00:04:02,879 --> 00:04:08,176
Then we subtract the 432 where
X's three marks made a line too,

59
00:04:08,176 --> 00:04:13,305
a row or column parallel to O's,
so X sneaked in a win first.

60
00:04:13,625 --> 00:04:16,541
That leaves 5,328 games.

61
00:04:16,725 --> 00:04:22,351
For games that end on moves 7, 8 and
9, it gets much worse: we'd have to

62
00:04:22,351 --> 00:04:28,446
check every earlier move for a win, and
a full board might be a win or a draw.

63
00:04:28,766 --> 00:04:30,553
It turns into a tangled mess.

64
00:04:30,792 --> 00:04:35,410
When counting gets this messy, there's
another plan. Don't count cleverly.

65
00:04:35,730 --> 00:04:41,635
Instead, teach a computer the
rules, and let it play every
possible game, one by one.

66
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:45,140
First, the computer needs a board.

67
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:47,968
We'll use a list of 9 squares.

68
00:04:48,288 --> 00:04:52,657
In Python, the programming language
we're using, the spots in a list

69
00:04:52,657 --> 00:04:57,027
are numbered starting from zero,
so our squares are numbered 0 to 8.

70
00:04:57,347 --> 00:04:58,926
An empty square holds a dot.

71
00:05:00,387 --> 00:05:03,507
Next, the computer needs to
know what counts as a win.

72
00:05:03,827 --> 00:05:10,259
We simply list all 8 winning lines,
as groups of three square numbers.

73
00:05:10,579 --> 00:05:13,906
Squares 0, 1 and 2 make the top row.

74
00:05:14,226 --> 00:05:17,556
Squares 0, 4 and 8 make a diagonal.

75
00:05:18,987 --> 00:05:24,519
Next comes a function: a little
mini-program with a name.

76
00:05:24,839 --> 00:05:29,423
This one, called winner, checks every line.

77
00:05:29,743 --> 00:05:33,154
If all three squares on a
line hold the same mark,

78
00:05:33,154 --> 00:05:36,361
and that mark isn't a
dot, that player has won.

79
00:05:36,681 --> 00:05:42,896
If no line matches, winner finds nothing,
and hands back None, which means nobody.

80
00:05:43,850 --> 00:05:49,687
Now for the clever part:
a function called explore.

81
00:05:50,007 --> 00:05:53,287
We tell it whose turn it
is, and its job is to count

82
00:05:53,287 --> 00:05:56,820
every game that can still
happen from the current board.

83
00:05:57,050 --> 00:05:59,552
It starts with two stopping rules.

84
00:05:59,872 --> 00:06:02,835
If someone has already
won, that's one finished

85
00:06:02,835 --> 00:06:05,420
game, so explore hands back the number 1.

86
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:10,698
If the board is full with no winner,
that's a draw, also one finished game.

87
00:06:10,917 --> 00:06:13,379
Otherwise, the game isn't over yet.

88
00:06:13,699 --> 00:06:16,459
So explore tries every empty square.

89
00:06:16,779 --> 00:06:20,661
It puts the player's mark there,
and then asks itself the same

90
00:06:20,661 --> 00:06:25,170
question, now for the other player:
how many games can happen from here?

91
00:06:25,490 --> 00:06:27,305
It adds up all the answers.

92
00:06:27,583 --> 00:06:31,100
Let's zoom into the first
of those, with O to move.

93
00:06:31,420 --> 00:06:35,324
If O takes the top-right
square, O wins: that's 1 game.

94
00:06:35,644 --> 00:06:38,788
Erase it, and try the
top-middle square instead.

95
00:06:39,107 --> 00:06:43,705
Then X fills the last square,
and it's a draw: 1 more.

96
00:06:44,025 --> 00:06:46,929
Undo both moves, and add up: 2 games.

97
00:06:47,249 --> 00:06:51,024
With the other two branches,
2 plus 1 plus 2 makes 5.

98
00:06:51,250 --> 00:06:54,472
When a function calls
itself, asking itself the

99
00:06:54,472 --> 00:06:57,694
same question like this,
it's called recursive.

100
00:06:58,014 --> 00:07:01,578
That might sound like it goes
on forever, but each call

101
00:07:01,578 --> 00:07:05,141
adds one more mark, so a
stopping rule always kicks in.

102
00:07:05,461 --> 00:07:07,467
Picture an upside-down tree.

103
00:07:07,787 --> 00:07:11,469
The empty board is at the
top, every possible move

104
00:07:11,469 --> 00:07:14,930
is a branch, and every
finished game is a leaf.

105
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:22,421
Explore walks down every branch, one at
a time, and counts the leaves it reaches.

106
00:07:22,650 --> 00:07:25,192
There's one more line,
and it's easy to miss.

107
00:07:25,512 --> 00:07:29,812
The program plays every game on just
one board, like a single whiteboard.

108
00:07:30,132 --> 00:07:33,967
So after exploring a move,
it erases that mark.

109
00:07:34,287 --> 00:07:39,776
That's called undoing the move, and
it means the next branch starts fresh.

110
00:07:40,096 --> 00:07:44,292
Trying a path, then stepping back to
try the next one, is called backtracking.

111
00:07:47,110 --> 00:07:51,067
A great way to understand a
program is to break it on purpose.

112
00:07:51,387 --> 00:07:56,137
Pause and ponder: what do you think
happens if we delete the undo line?

113
00:08:06,377 --> 00:08:07,860
The program prints just 3.

114
00:08:08,180 --> 00:08:10,762
Without undo, nothing ever gets erased.

115
00:08:11,082 --> 00:08:14,578
The program plays one
game, filling the squares

116
00:08:14,578 --> 00:08:17,926
in order, until X makes
a diagonal on move 7.

117
00:08:18,246 --> 00:08:19,387
That's 1.

118
00:08:19,707 --> 00:08:24,504
Then it tries X's other choices,
but the old X is still there.

119
00:08:24,824 --> 00:08:27,936
X lands on the bottom-middle
square, and the

120
00:08:27,936 --> 00:08:31,118
old diagonal counts as
a win again: that's 2.

121
00:08:31,438 --> 00:08:34,101
Then the bottom-right square: that's 3.

122
00:08:34,421 --> 00:08:37,293
Now the board is full, so
there's nothing left to try.

123
00:08:37,510 --> 00:08:40,854
Now put undo back, and
delete the winner check

124
00:08:40,854 --> 00:08:44,562
instead, so games only stop
when the board is full.

125
00:08:44,882 --> 00:08:47,049
Pause and predict the answer.

126
00:08:57,243 --> 00:08:59,481
362,880.

127
00:08:59,801 --> 00:09:01,292
That's nine factorial!

128
00:09:01,612 --> 00:09:05,658
Without the winner check,
the program keeps playing

129
00:09:05,658 --> 00:09:09,307
after a win, so it counts
all the ghost games.

130
00:09:09,627 --> 00:09:12,801
It rediscovered our very first count.

131
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:17,954
Now let's put every line back
and run the real program.

132
00:09:18,274 --> 00:09:22,244
After a moment, it prints 255,168.

133
00:09:22,440 --> 00:09:26,704
Let's split that total
by when each game ends.

134
00:09:27,025 --> 00:09:34,983
1,440 end on move 5, and 5,328 on move
6, the same numbers we found by hand.

135
00:09:35,303 --> 00:09:42,912
Then the bars shoot up: about 48 thousand
end on move 7, and 73 thousand on move 8.

136
00:09:43,232 --> 00:09:47,327
Just over half of all
games use all 9 moves:

137
00:09:47,327 --> 00:09:51,421
about 82 thousand X
wins, plus 46,080 draws.

138
00:09:51,741 --> 00:09:54,885
Together, that's 255,168.

139
00:09:55,107 --> 00:09:58,455
And here's how it connects
to nine factorial.

140
00:09:58,774 --> 00:10:02,639
Remember, nine factorial
counted each game that ends on

141
00:10:02,639 --> 00:10:06,504
move 5 a total of 24 times,
once for each ghost ending.

142
00:10:06,824 --> 00:10:12,432
A game ending on move 6 was counted
6 times, and on move 7, twice.

143
00:10:12,752 --> 00:10:16,275
Games that end on move
8 or 9 have at most one

144
00:10:16,275 --> 00:10:19,720
empty square, so they
were counted just once.

145
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:26,356
Multiply each bar by its number,
add them up, and you get
exactly nine factorial again.

146
00:10:29,173 --> 00:10:30,703
Who wins the most games?

147
00:10:31,023 --> 00:10:39,621
X wins 131,184 of them, O wins
77,904, and 46,080 are draws.

148
00:10:39,941 --> 00:10:43,084
X wins in more than half
of all possible games,

149
00:10:43,084 --> 00:10:46,294
because going first gives
X more chances to win.

150
00:10:46,507 --> 00:10:47,660
One last puzzle.

151
00:10:47,980 --> 00:10:50,738
Which first move for X leads to the most

152
00:10:50,738 --> 00:10:54,184
different games: a corner,
an edge, or the center?

153
00:10:54,504 --> 00:10:56,299
Pause and guess.

154
00:11:06,507 --> 00:11:14,191
Surprise: the edge, with 29,592 games.

155
00:11:14,511 --> 00:11:21,050
Each corner gives 27,732, and the
center the fewest, just 25,872.

156
00:11:21,370 --> 00:11:27,927
Why? The center sits on 4 winning lines,
a corner on 3, and an edge on just 2.

157
00:11:28,247 --> 00:11:32,593
More lines give X more chances
to win early, on move 5 or

158
00:11:32,593 --> 00:11:36,939
7, and every early win chops
whole branches off the tree.

159
00:11:37,173 --> 00:11:41,273
But careful: more games
doesn't mean a better move.

160
00:11:41,593 --> 00:11:46,323
Out of all the games that start in
the center, X wins about 6 in 10.

161
00:11:46,643 --> 00:11:49,464
Starting on an edge, X
wins fewer than half.

162
00:11:51,503 --> 00:11:54,586
So does going first mean X always wins?

163
00:11:54,906 --> 00:11:58,662
No. A computer can do more than count.

164
00:11:58,982 --> 00:12:04,853
It starts at the bottom of the tree, where
every game is finished, and works upward.

165
00:12:05,173 --> 00:12:08,878
At every branch it picks
the best move: best

166
00:12:08,878 --> 00:12:12,921
for X on X's turns, and
best for O on O's turns.

167
00:12:13,241 --> 00:12:16,471
Doing that, it has solved
tic-tac-toe: if both

168
00:12:16,471 --> 00:12:19,982
players play perfectly,
every game ends in a draw.

169
00:12:20,302 --> 00:12:24,745
So every one of X's wins needs
a mistake by O somewhere.

170
00:12:27,170 --> 00:12:29,346
Could we do the same for chess?

171
00:12:29,666 --> 00:12:30,831
Not a chance.

172
00:12:31,151 --> 00:12:35,810
In 1950, the mathematician and
engineer Claude Shannon estimated

173
00:12:35,810 --> 00:12:40,324
that chess has at least 10 to
the power of 120 possible games.

174
00:12:40,644 --> 00:12:43,364
That's a 1 followed by 120 zeros!

175
00:12:43,684 --> 00:12:48,701
The whole observable universe has
only about 10 to the power of 80 atoms.

176
00:12:48,903 --> 00:12:52,918
So chess programs can't
explore every branch.

177
00:12:53,238 --> 00:12:57,350
They look only so many moves
ahead, then estimate who's winning,

178
00:12:57,350 --> 00:13:00,949
using what they learned from
millions of practice games.

179
00:13:01,269 --> 00:13:05,039
But underneath, it's the
same idea you just learned:

180
00:13:05,039 --> 00:13:08,447
a tree of moves, explored
one branch at a time.

181
00:13:09,236 --> 00:13:10,573
Let's recap.

182
00:13:10,893 --> 00:13:17,743
Multiplying the choices gives
nine factorial, 362,880 orders.

183
00:13:18,063 --> 00:13:24,744
But games stop when someone wins, so
many of those orders are ghost games.

184
00:13:25,064 --> 00:13:29,204
And a short recursive
program counts every real

185
00:13:29,204 --> 00:13:33,520
game by trying a move,
exploring, and undoing it.

186
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:36,963
The answer is exactly 255,168.

187
00:13:37,170 --> 00:13:38,842
Now it's your turn.

188
00:13:39,162 --> 00:13:43,745
Change the program so it counts
only the games X wins, or only

189
00:13:43,745 --> 00:13:48,625
the games that end on move 7, or
see what happens if O goes first.

190
00:13:48,945 --> 00:13:52,376
The program and the challenges
are linked in the description.

191
00:13:52,696 --> 00:13:54,032
Have fun exploring!
